WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.333 --> 00:00:04.733
Do you think this video will be 
on the Internet in 2070?

00:00:05.566 --> 00:00:08.166
Maybe in some... Internet archive 
somewhere?

00:00:08.866 --> 00:00:10.700
Well, researchers of the future

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.066
if you were wondering what 
we were doing

00:00:12.066 --> 00:00:14.266
here in the 2020s about 
climate change

00:00:14.266 --> 00:00:16.133
the answer is we were committing

00:00:16.166 --> 00:00:18.000
to net zero.

00:00:19.066 --> 00:00:20.300
In the past few years

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.200
more than 70 countries, 
one after the next...

00:00:22.466 --> 00:00:25.133
have made a pledge to reach 
net zero emissions.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.133
That includes places that have barely 
contributed to climate warming...

00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:30.366
and it includes all of the countries 

00:00:30.366 --> 00:00:33.366
that have been most responsible 
for emissions so far.

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:36.666
It seems like everyone 
got the message

00:00:36.666 --> 00:00:40.500
that humankind needs to become 
carbon neutral

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.872
and we need to do it 
in the next 3 to 5 decades.

00:00:44.366 --> 00:00:45.300
But that would have been easier

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.533
if we had started cutting emissions 
20 years ago

00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:48.900
because now we're up here

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.666
and the math is getting 
really tough.

00:00:51.366 --> 00:00:52.233
So to make it work

00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:54.800
governments are penciling in 
"negative emissions."

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.133
That's the net part of the 
net zero promise.

00:00:58.166 --> 00:00:59.800
The idea that we can reach zero

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.533
while still emitting greenhouse gases

00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:03.800
if we're also taking CO2 
out of the air.

00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:05.400
It's a bit contentious

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.966
but we're going to be hearing 
a lot about it.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:08.166
-It's called CDR.
-CDR.

00:01:08.166 --> 00:01:10.033
So carbon dioxide removal.

00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:13.633
What we want to do is put that carbon 
back where it belongs.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.333
Giana Amador is 
a CDR advocate

00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:19.433
and she's going to help us explain this 
with the bathtub metaphor.

00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:22.866
The bathtub in this situation 
is our atmosphere

00:01:22.866 --> 00:01:25.200
and the water in the bathtub 
is the CO2

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.533
that's in our atmosphere.

00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:27.900
Before the Industrial Revolution

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.866
the land and ocean typically released
as much CO2 into the air

00:01:30.866 --> 00:01:33.066
as they absorbed back down 
through this drain.

00:01:33.666 --> 00:01:35.666
But when people started 
digging up coal and oil

00:01:35.666 --> 00:01:39.033
to power their machines, first in Europe 
and then in the US

00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:41.800
it sent this new flow of CO2 
into the air

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.100
one that the plants and oceans 
couldn't keep up with.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.233
About 45% of those emissions 
stay in the atmosphere

00:01:49.233 --> 00:01:51.400
trapping heat and setting us up

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.166
for an escalating array of 
climate disasters.

00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:58.666
So in 2015, all of the countries 
send someone off to Paris

00:01:58.666 --> 00:02:01.200
to say, “Okay, everyone, we need 
to keep warming

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.900
well below two degrees Celsius.”

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.900
And really we're aiming 
for 1.5 degrees.

00:02:05.233 --> 00:02:07.166
That's above pre-industrial levels.

00:02:07.166 --> 00:02:09.866
And the exact numbers matter 
less than the fact that

00:02:09.866 --> 00:02:12.500
every increment of warming 
increases risk.

00:02:12.533 --> 00:02:14.800
So first and foremost, 
we need to turn off the tap.

00:02:15.066 --> 00:02:16.466
Everything else is futile

00:02:16.466 --> 00:02:18.500
if we don't stop the root 
of the problem today.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:19.633
Turn off that tap.

00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:21.500
Stop emitting CO2.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.333
Not only have we not 
turned off any taps.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.733
Global emissions are still rising, 
especially in Asia...

00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:29.433
where, you know, 60% 
of human beings live

00:02:29.433 --> 00:02:32.200
many of whom are just emerging 
out of poverty.

00:02:32.533 --> 00:02:35.700
So now we're here at nearly 
1.2 degrees of warming.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.766
Frankly, we have not done
what it would take

00:02:39.766 --> 00:02:41.500
to meet that 1.5 degree goal.

00:02:42.633 --> 00:02:44.766
But we may be able to keep 
warming under two degrees

00:02:44.766 --> 00:02:46.700
if all of those net zero promises

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.433
become real net zero policies.

00:02:48.766 --> 00:02:50.800
And that's where things 
get a little tricky.

00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:54.466
Turning these emissions all the way off 
could get really hard

00:02:54.466 --> 00:02:56.700
really expensive or even unfair.

00:02:56.866 --> 00:02:57.966
So everyone's kind of hoping

00:02:57.966 --> 00:03:00.000
that we can work from the 
bottom of the tub

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.133
to... kind of increase the size of the drain

00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:05.233
increase the speed at which we can drain 
that water from the bathtub.

00:03:05.433 --> 00:03:07.700
Now, CDR is distinct 
from CCS

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.533
which prevents emissions at the source. 
That works on the faucet.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.966
CDR grabs CO2 from the air

00:03:12.966 --> 00:03:15.966
and people have come up with a lot of 
interesting ways of doing that.

00:03:15.966 --> 00:03:19.266
The most common one so far is 
planting and protecting forests.

00:03:19.533 --> 00:03:21.900
It's one of the more affordable 
CDR approaches right now

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.600
and it has a lot of benefits 
aside from capturing CO2.

00:03:24.933 --> 00:03:28.100
But we've seen from problems 
with the carbon offsets market

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.733
that it can be hard to verify.

00:03:30.066 --> 00:03:32.266
And, you know, trees 
don't live forever.

00:03:32.633 --> 00:03:35.133
So most of the modeling scenarios 
that limit warming

00:03:35.133 --> 00:03:37.833
assume we’ll also be able 
to build a new drain

00:03:37.866 --> 00:03:39.900
for more permanent 
carbon storage.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.666
So through things like rock weathering,
we can crush up a bunch of rocks

00:03:44.666 --> 00:03:47.666
that naturally react with CO2 and 
try to speed up that reaction.

00:03:47.933 --> 00:03:50.300
Or we can use chemistry 
to mimic...

00:03:50.666 --> 00:03:53.166
natural processes through technologies
like direct air capture

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:56.666
which basically use a type of chemical 
that selectively binds

00:03:56.666 --> 00:03:58.800
with CO2 to capture it 
from the air.

00:03:59.166 --> 00:04:01.066
These techniques all come 
with different costs

00:04:01.066 --> 00:04:03.200
and they're in really early stages 
of development.

00:04:03.566 --> 00:04:06.433
So there's a concern that the 
people in charge of the faucets

00:04:06.433 --> 00:04:09.000
will see any movement 
down here as an excuse

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.066
to avoid making cuts 
up there.

00:04:11.433 --> 00:04:12.500
Should we be counting on

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.533
 large amounts of carbon removal 
for our climate plans?

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:17.600
Well, it seems we already are.

00:04:19.333 --> 00:04:20.900
The US government's net zero plan 

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:24.133
calls for 500 million metric tons 
of CO2 removal 

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:26.900
by 2050, up from 
zero today.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.200
Model simulations

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.066
that limit warming to 1.5 degrees

00:04:30.066 --> 00:04:32.933
envision around 3.8 billion tons
of permanent

00:04:32.933 --> 00:04:34.933
carbon removal globally every year.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:36.833
And this...

00:04:36.833 --> 00:04:38.866
is the total amount of permanent 
carbon removal 

00:04:38.866 --> 00:04:40.500
that has happened so far.

00:04:41.233 --> 00:04:43.233
So would you call that 
wishful thinking?

00:04:43.733 --> 00:04:47.333
Or is it foresight about how much 
can happen in 30 years

00:04:47.333 --> 00:04:50.500
if we start our way up 
the learning curve now?

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.733
We have actually a really 
strong playbook

00:04:52.733 --> 00:04:55.433
of developing technologies and 
driving down costs.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.633
If we go all the way 
back to the 1970s

00:04:58.633 --> 00:05:03.533
we see technologies like solar PV 
be around $70 per watt.

00:05:03.566 --> 00:05:04.933
Fast forward to 2023.

00:05:04.933 --> 00:05:08.466
We see those prices down 
at around $0.07 per watt

00:05:08.466 --> 00:05:11.500
or even lower and at price parity 
with fossil fuels.

00:05:11.733 --> 00:05:13.266
The challenge with that 

00:05:13.266 --> 00:05:15.066
is that renewable energy produces

00:05:15.066 --> 00:05:18.933
something that's quite useful, 
which is energy and CDR...

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.466
What is the customer for CDR?

00:05:22.533 --> 00:05:24.366
Where does the demand 
come from?

00:05:24.366 --> 00:05:26.266
You've seen some major companies

00:05:26.266 --> 00:05:30.466
including Stripe, Meta, Alphabet, 
McKinsey, Microsoft...

00:05:30.766 --> 00:05:32.833
All of these companies 
have made commitments

00:05:32.833 --> 00:05:35.766
to purchase carbon dioxide removal 
as part of their 

00:05:35.766 --> 00:05:38.100
corporate social responsibility plans.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.000
That being said, I think 
in the long term

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.300
carbon removal could be provided 
as a public service

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.366
in the ways that that we manage 
some of our other pollution.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.700
We pay for someone to come around
to collect our trash

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:52.400
and to manage it in a way that 
it's safe for our communities.

00:05:52.466 --> 00:05:55.433
The US government is already 
offering billions of dollars

00:05:55.433 --> 00:05:58.166
to try to get a direct air capture industry 
off the ground.

00:05:58.666 --> 00:06:00.066
And I think it makes sense for the countries

00:06:00.066 --> 00:06:03.233
that have used the most fossil fuels 
to do the work of finding out

00:06:03.233 --> 00:06:04.466
if CDR can scale.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:07.533
But if it doesn't...

00:06:07.833 --> 00:06:10.800
well, then net zero will have been 
just another way

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.300
of kicking the can down the road.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.800
Which, of course, is how we ended up 
here in the first place.

00:06:15.266 --> 00:06:18.333
In this position where we may now 
have no choice...

00:06:18.933 --> 00:06:20.466
but to try everything.

